            "text": "{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"missing_hyp\",\n      \"location\": \"fadd : ∀ x y, f (x + y) = f x * f y - g x * g y\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The informal problem also assumes g(x+y) = f(x)g(y) + g(x)f(y), but the Lean hypothesis contains only the equation for f. This omission may affect the conclusion: f(x) = exp(x)(1−x) and g(x) = x exp(x) satisfy the stated hypotheses, including f′(0) = 0, but their squared values sum to exp(2) at x = 1.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"The original fadd formula and the edited formula should disagree for these functions: the f equation holds, while the g equation fails, for example at x = y = 1.\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"difference\",\n      \"comparison\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": null,\n      \"edit_from\": \"∀ x y, f (x + y) = f x * f y - g x * g y\",\n      \"edit_to\": \"(∀ x y, f (x + y) = f x * f y - g x * g y) ∧ (∀ x y, g (x + y) = f x * g y + g x * f y)\",\n      \"bridge\": \"translated\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Verify the proposed functions and the failed conclusion in Lean, and confirm that the added equation is the intended translation of the informal assumption.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The Lean proposition omits the informal problem’s addition equation for g; a concrete counterexample appears to make the resulting proposition false.\"\n}"
